(* Content-type: application/vnd.wolfram.mathematica *)
(* The Laplacian and Curvature - Computational Classnotes - CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 *)

Notebook[{
Cell["The Laplacian and Curvature", "Title"],
Cell["Instances and relationships, computed \:2014 a Laplacian Lab companion notebook", "Subtitle"],
Cell["\:00a9 2026 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"],
Cell["Each section below matches a section of the write-up \:201cThe Laplacian and Curvature \:2014 Instances and Relationships\:201d. Evaluate the notebook top to bottom: Sections 2\:20136 use the toolkit of Section 1 and Section 9 uses the functions of Section 8. Each section ends with a check that should return True, 0, or a number close to the value stated in the write-up. Conventions: \:0394 is the analyst\:2019s Laplacian (\:0394 = div grad, negative semidefinite on closed manifolds); on graphs \:0394f(x) = mean of f over the neighbours of x minus f(x) (random-walk normalisation) unless stated otherwise.", "Text"],
Cell["1. Riemannian toolkit (used in Sections 2\:20135)", "Section"],
Cell["Laplace\:2013Beltrami operator, Christoffel symbols, covariant Hessian, Riemann tensor and Gauss curvature for a metric g in coordinates \|01d4cd.", "Text"],
Cell["ClearAll[christoffel, laplaceBeltrami, gradNorm2, hessian, hessNorm2, gaussCurvature];\nchristoffel[g_, \|01d4cd_] := Module[{gi = Inverse[g], n = Length[\|01d4cd]},\n  Table[1/2 Sum[gi[[i, l]] (D[g[[l, j]], \|01d4cd[[k]]] + D[g[[l, k]], \|01d4cd[[j]]] - D[g[[j, k]], \|01d4cd[[l]]]), {l, n}],\n   {i, n}, {j, n}, {k, n}] // Simplify];\nlaplaceBeltrami[g_, \|01d4cd_][u_] := Module[{gi = Inverse[g], s = Sqrt[Det[g]], n = Length[\|01d4cd]},\n  1/s Sum[D[s gi[[i, j]] D[u, \|01d4cd[[j]]], \|01d4cd[[i]]], {i, n}, {j, n}]];\ngradNorm2[g_, \|01d4cd_][u_] := With[{gi = Inverse[g]}, Sum[gi[[i, j]] D[u, \|01d4cd[[i]]] D[u, \|01d4cd[[j]]], {i, Length[\|01d4cd]}, {j, Length[\|01d4cd]}]];\nhessian[g_, \|01d4cd_][u_] := With[{\:0393 = christoffel[g, \|01d4cd], n = Length[\|01d4cd]},\n  Table[D[u, \|01d4cd[[i]], \|01d4cd[[j]]] - Sum[\:0393[[k, i, j]] D[u, \|01d4cd[[k]]], {k, n}], {i, n}, {j, n}]];\nhessNorm2[g_, \|01d4cd_][u_] := With[{gi = Inverse[g], H = hessian[g, \|01d4cd][u]}, Tr[gi . H . gi . H]];\ngaussCurvature[g_, \|01d4cd_] := Module[{\:0393 = christoffel[g, \|01d4cd], R},\n  R[i_, j_, k_, l_] := D[\:0393[[i, j, l]], \|01d4cd[[k]]] - D[\:0393[[i, j, k]], \|01d4cd[[l]]] +\n     Sum[\:0393[[i, k, m]] \:0393[[m, j, l]] - \:0393[[i, l, m]] \:0393[[m, j, k]], {m, 2}];\n  Simplify[Sum[g[[1, i]] R[i, 2, 1, 2], {i, 2}]/Det[g]]];\ngS2 = {{1, 0}, {0, Sin[\:03b8]^2}}; \|01d4cdS2 = {\:03b8, \:03c6};\ngaussCurvature[gS2, \|01d4cdS2]", "Input"],
Cell["1.1 Curvatures of an embedded surface, and a colour scale", "Subsection"],
Cell["For a parametrised surface X(u, v) in \:211d\:00b3: first fundamental form E, F, G, second fundamental form L, M, N, K = (LN \:2212 M\:00b2)/(EG \:2212 F\:00b2), H = (EN \:2212 2FM + GL)/(2(EG \:2212 F\:00b2)). Colours below: blue = negative curvature, near-white = zero, orange = positive.", "Text"],
Cell["surfaceCurvatures[pos_, \|01d4cd_] := Module[{Xu, Xv, nn, E1, F1, G1, L1, M1, N1},\n  Xu = D[pos, \|01d4cd[[1]]]; Xv = D[pos, \|01d4cd[[2]]];\n  nn = Cross[Xu, Xv]/Sqrt[Cross[Xu, Xv] . Cross[Xu, Xv]];\n  {E1, F1, G1} = {Xu . Xu, Xu . Xv, Xv . Xv};\n  {L1, M1, N1} = {D[pos, \|01d4cd[[1]], \|01d4cd[[1]]] . nn, D[pos, \|01d4cd[[1]], \|01d4cd[[2]]] . nn, D[pos, \|01d4cd[[2]], \|01d4cd[[2]]] . nn};\n  <|\"K\" -> Simplify[(L1 N1 - M1^2)/(E1 G1 - F1^2)],\n    \"H\" -> Simplify[(E1 N1 - 2 F1 M1 + G1 L1)/(2 (E1 G1 - F1^2))],\n    \"dA\" -> Simplify[Sqrt[E1 G1 - F1^2]]|>];\n\:03baColour[k_, s_] := Blend[{RGBColor[0.18, 0.36, 0.78], GrayLevel[0.96], RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17]}, (1 + Clip[k/s, {-1, 1}])/2];\ncurvatureSurface[pos_, {u_, u0_, u1_}, {v_, v0_, v1_}, \:03ba_, s_, label_, opts___] :=\n  ParametricPlot3D[pos, {u, u0, u1}, {v, v0, v1},\n   ColorFunction -> Function[{x, y, z, uu, vv}, \:03baColour[\:03ba /. {u -> uu, v -> vv}, s]], ColorFunctionScaling -> False,\n   Mesh -> 12, MeshStyle -> GrayLevel[0.5, 0.35], Lighting -> \"Neutral\", Boxed -> False, Axes -> False,\n   PlotPoints -> 40, PlotLabel -> Style[label, 11], opts];\n\:03baLegend[s_, name_] := BarLegend[{\:03baColour[#, s] &, {-s, s}}, LegendLabel -> name];\nsurfaceCurvatures[{(2 + Cos[v]) Cos[u], (2 + Cos[v]) Sin[u], Sin[v]}, {u, v}]", "Input"],
Cell["2. Gauss curvature is a Laplacian: conformal metrics and Liouville\:2019s equation", "Section"],
Cell["For g = e^{2u}(dx\:00b2 + dy\:00b2) the Gauss curvature is K = \:2212e^{\:22122u} \:0394\:2080u, where \:0394\:2080 is the flat Laplacian. Equivalently \:0394\:2080u = \:2212K e^{2u} (Liouville\:2019s equation when K is constant). Checks: the round sphere through stereographic projection gives K = 1, the Poincar\:00e9 half-plane gives K = \:22121, and the formula agrees with the Riemann-tensor computation for a generic u.", "Text"],
Cell["Kconformal[u_] := Simplify[-Exp[-2 u] (D[u, {x, 2}] + D[u, {y, 2}])];\n{Kconformal[Log[2/(1 + x^2 + y^2)]],          (* unit sphere, stereographic *)\n Kconformal[Log[1/y]],                         (* hyperbolic half-plane *)\n Kconformal[Log[2/(1 - x^2 - y^2)]],           (* Poincar\:00e9 disc *)\n Simplify[Kconformal[w[x, y]] - gaussCurvature[Exp[2 w[x, y]] IdentityMatrix[2], {x, y}]]}", "Input"],
Cell["2.1 Worked examples: positive, zero and negative Gauss curvature", "Subsection"],
Cell["Six classical surfaces, each coloured by its Gauss curvature K. The sphere is K = 1 everywhere; the cylinder is K = 0 (it unrolls flat); the torus is positive on the outside, negative on the inside and zero on the top and bottom circles; the saddle, catenoid and pseudosphere are negatively curved, the pseudosphere with K = \:22121 everywhere.", "Text"],
Cell["surfaces = {\n   {\"Sphere\", {Sin[v] Cos[u], Sin[v] Sin[u], Cos[v]}, {0, 2 \:03c0}, {0.01, \:03c0 - 0.01}},\n   {\"Cylinder\", {Cos[u], Sin[u], v}, {0, 2 \:03c0}, {-1, 1}},\n   {\"Torus (R = 2, r = 1)\", {(2 + Cos[v]) Cos[u], (2 + Cos[v]) Sin[u], Sin[v]}, {0, 2 \:03c0}, {0, 2 \:03c0}},\n   {\"Saddle z = x\:00b2 \:2212 y\:00b2\", {u, v, u^2 - v^2}, {-1, 1}, {-1, 1}},\n   {\"Catenoid\", {Cosh[v] Cos[u], Cosh[v] Sin[u], v}, {0, 2 \:03c0}, {-1.2, 1.2}},\n   {\"Pseudosphere\", {Sech[v] Cos[u], Sech[v] Sin[u], v - Tanh[v]}, {0, 2 \:03c0}, {0.05, 3}}};\nKformulas = Table[{s[[1]], surfaceCurvatures[s[[2]], {u, v}][\"K\"]}, {s, surfaces}];\nTableForm[Kformulas, TableHeadings -> {None, {\"surface\", \"K(u, v)\"}}]", "Input"],
Cell["Legended[GraphicsGrid[Partition[Table[With[{s = surfaces[[i]], K = Kformulas[[i, 2]]},\n     curvatureSurface[s[[2]], {u, s[[3, 1]], s[[3, 2]]}, {v, s[[4, 1]], s[[4, 2]]}, K, 1, s[[1]]]], {i, 6}], 3],\n   ImageSize -> 760, Spacings -> {10, 10}], \:03baLegend[1, \"K\"]]", "Input"],
Cell["Gauss\:2013Bonnet on the examples: \:222cK dA = 2\:03c0\:03c7. The torus (\:03c7 = 0) integrates to 0, the sphere (\:03c7 = 2) to 4\:03c0. The Liouville form of Section 2 reproduces the sphere\:2019s K from the stereographic conformal factor.", "Text"],
Cell["With[{t = surfaceCurvatures[{(2 + Cos[v]) Cos[u], (2 + Cos[v]) Sin[u], Sin[v]}, {u, v}],\n   s = surfaceCurvatures[{Sin[v] Cos[u], Sin[v] Sin[u], Cos[v]}, {u, v}]},\n {\"torus \:222cK dA\" -> Integrate[Simplify[t[\"K\"] t[\"dA\"], 0 < v < 2 \:03c0], {u, 0, 2 \:03c0}, {v, 0, 2 \:03c0}],\n  \"sphere \:222cK dA\" -> Integrate[Simplify[s[\"K\"] s[\"dA\"], 0 < v < \:03c0], {u, 0, 2 \:03c0}, {v, 0, \:03c0}]}]", "Input"],
Cell["3. Mean curvature is a Laplacian: \:0394X = \:22122H\|01d427", "Section"],
Cell["For a surface X : U \:2192 \:211d\:00b3 with induced metric, the Laplace\:2013Beltrami operator applied to each coordinate of the position vector gives the mean-curvature vector: \:0394X = \:22122H\|01d427 (H the mean of the principal curvatures, \|01d427 the unit normal; the sign depends on orientation). Consequence: a surface is minimal exactly when its coordinate functions are harmonic. Checks: sphere (H = 1/a), torus, and the catenoid (H = 0).", "Text"],
Cell["meanCurvatureVector[pos_, \|01d4cd_, assum_ : True] := Module[{g, n, \:0394X},\n  g = Simplify[Table[D[pos, \|01d4cd[[i]]] . D[pos, \|01d4cd[[j]]], {i, 2}, {j, 2}]];\n  \:0394X = Simplify[laplaceBeltrami[g, \|01d4cd] /@ pos];\n  n = Simplify[Normalize[Cross[D[pos, \|01d4cd[[1]]], D[pos, \|01d4cd[[2]]]]], \|01d4cd \:2208 Reals && assum];\n  <|\"H\" -> Simplify[-\:0394X . n/2, \|01d4cd \:2208 Reals && assum],\n    \"tangential part\" -> Simplify[{\:0394X . D[pos, \|01d4cd[[1]]], \:0394X . D[pos, \|01d4cd[[2]]]}]|>];\n{meanCurvatureVector[3 {Sin[\:03b8] Cos[\:03c6], Sin[\:03b8] Sin[\:03c6], Cos[\:03b8]}, {\:03b8, \:03c6}, 0 < \:03b8 < \:03c0],\n meanCurvatureVector[{(2 + Cos[v]) Cos[u], (2 + Cos[v]) Sin[u], Sin[v]}, {u, v}],\n meanCurvatureVector[{Cosh[v] Cos[u], Cosh[v] Sin[u], v}, {u, v}]}", "Input"],
Cell["3.1 Worked examples: the mean-curvature vector drawn", "Subsection"],
Cell["Left: the torus coloured by H, with \:2212\:0394X drawn as arrows (\:0394X itself points into the tube). The arrows are normal to the surface, longest on the outer equator where H = 2/3, and vanish on the inner equator where H = 0. Right: the catenoid and the helicoid are minimal surfaces (H = 0): their coordinate functions are harmonic, so \:0394X vanishes and no arrows appear.", "Text"],
Cell["torusPos = {(2 + Cos[v]) Cos[u], (2 + Cos[v]) Sin[u], Sin[v]};\ngT = Simplify[Table[D[torusPos, {u, v}[[i]]] . D[torusPos, {u, v}[[j]]], {i, 2}, {j, 2}]];\n\:0394Xtorus = Simplify[laplaceBeltrami[gT, {u, v}] /@ torusPos];\nHtorus = Simplify[-surfaceCurvatures[torusPos, {u, v}][\"H\"], v \:2208 Reals];   (* outward normal *)\narrows = Table[With[{p = torusPos /. {u -> a, v -> b}, w = \:0394Xtorus /. {u -> a, v -> b}}, Arrow[{p, p - 0.3 w}]],\n   {a, 0, 2 \:03c0 - 0.01, 2 \:03c0/16}, {b, 0, 2 \:03c0 - 0.01, 2 \:03c0/8}];\ntorusPlot = Show[curvatureSurface[torusPos, {u, 0, 2 \:03c0}, {v, 0, 2 \:03c0}, Htorus, 0.7, \"Torus: colour = H, arrows = \:2212\:0394X = 2H n\"],\n   Graphics3D[{Darker[Gray], Arrowheads[0.012], Flatten[arrows]}]];\nminimal = {curvatureSurface[{Cosh[v] Cos[u], Cosh[v] Sin[u], v}, {u, 0, 2 \:03c0}, {v, -1.2, 1.2},\n    surfaceCurvatures[{Cosh[v] Cos[u], Cosh[v] Sin[u], v}, {u, v}][\"H\"], 0.7, \"Catenoid: H = 0\"],\n   curvatureSurface[{v Cos[u], v Sin[u], u/2}, {u, 0, 2 \:03c0}, {v, -1.5, 1.5},\n    surfaceCurvatures[{v Cos[u], v Sin[u], u/2}, {u, v}][\"H\"], 0.7, \"Helicoid: H = 0\", ViewPoint -> {2.5, -1.5, 1}]};\n{Htorus, Legended[GraphicsRow[Prepend[minimal, torusPlot], ImageSize -> 760], \:03baLegend[0.7, \"H\"]]}", "Input"],
Cell["4. Mean values and volumes: curvature in the mean-value expansion of \:0394", "Section"],
Cell["Laplacian Lab Section 1 derives \:0394 from the sphere mean in flat space: M_r f = f + r\:00b2/(2n) \:0394f + \:2026. On a curved manifold the same expansion picks up curvature (Gray\:2013Willmore 1982). On a surface of constant curvature K the geodesic-circle mean is", "Text"],
Cell["  M_r f(p) = f + (r\:00b2/4) \:0394f + r\:2074 (\:0394\:00b2f/64 + K \:0394f/96) + r\:2076 (\:0394\:00b3f/2304 + K \:0394\:00b2f/1152 + K\:00b2 \:0394f/1440) + O(r\:2078).", "Text"],
Cell["Derivation on the unit sphere: the circle-mean operator acts on a degree-l spherical harmonic as the Legendre polynomial P_l(cos r), and \:0394 acts as \:03bb = \:2212l(l+1). Matching the series of P_l(cos r) in powers of \:03bb gives the coefficients; scaling the sphere restores K.", "Text"],
Cell["\:03bb = -l (l + 1);\nser = Expand@Normal@Series[LegendreP[l, Cos[r]], {r, 0, 6}];\n{Factor@Coefficient[ser, r, 2],\n SolveAlways[Coefficient[ser, r, 4] == a \:03bb^2 + b \:03bb, l],\n SolveAlways[Coefficient[ser, r, 6] == a3 \:03bb^3 + b3 \:03bb^2 + c3 \:03bb, l]}", "Input"],
Cell["Direct check on S\:00b2 with a non-symmetric test function. Write it in ambient coordinates and extend it as a degree-0 function F(X/|X|); then |X|\:00b2\:00b7\:0394_\:211d\:00b3 of it is again degree 0 and equals \:0394_S\:00b2 F. Average F over the geodesic circle of colatitude r about the north pole and compare with the formula evaluated at the pole.", "Text"],
Cell["F0[{x_, y_, z_}] := Exp[x] (1 + z)^2 + y z^3;\next = F0[{x, y, z}/Sqrt[x^2 + y^2 + z^2]];\n\:0394S2amb[h_] := Simplify[(x^2 + y^2 + z^2) Laplacian[h, {x, y, z}]];\nL1 = \:0394S2amb[ext]; L2 = \:0394S2amb[L1]; L3 = \:0394S2amb[L2];\natPole[h_] := h /. {x -> 0, y -> 0, z -> 1};\ncircleMean = Integrate[Normal@Series[F0[{Sin[r] Cos[\:03c6], Sin[r] Sin[\:03c6], Cos[r]}], {r, 0, 6}], {\:03c6, 0, 2 \:03c0}]/(2 \:03c0);\nformula = atPole[ext] + r^2/4 atPole[L1] + r^4 (atPole[L2]/64 + atPole[L1]/96) +\n   r^6 (atPole[L3]/2304 + atPole[L2]/1152 + atPole[L1]/1440);\nSimplify[Expand[circleMean - formula]]", "Input"],
Cell["Volumes. Area of a geodesic disc on a surface of curvature K: A(r) = \:03c0r\:00b2(1 \:2212 K r\:00b2/12 + K\:00b2r\:2074/360 \:2212 \:2026) (Bertrand\:2013Diguet\:2013Puiseux). In dimension n: Vol B_r = \:03c9_n r\:207f (1 \:2212 S r\:00b2/(6(n+2)) + O(r\:2074)), S the scalar curvature. Check on S\:00b3 (S = 6).", "Text"],
Cell["areaK = 2 \:03c0 Integrate[Sin[Sqrt[K] \:03c1]/Sqrt[K], {\:03c1, 0, r}];\nvolS3 = 4 \:03c0 Integrate[Sin[\:03c1]^2, {\:03c1, 0, r}];\n{Series[areaK/(\:03c0 r^2), {r, 0, 4}], Series[volS3/(4/3 \:03c0 r^3), {r, 0, 2}], 1 - 6 r^2/(6 (3 + 2))}", "Input"],
Cell["4.1 Pictures: geodesic circles, and where the flat formula fails", "Subsection"],
Cell["Left: the test function on S\:00b2 with geodesic circles about the north pole at r = 0.3, 0.6, \:2026, 1.5. Right: the error of three approximations to the exact circle mean, on log\:2013log axes. The flat-space formula (K = 0) misses the K\:0394f/96 term, so its error falls only like r\:2074. Adding that term makes the error fall like r\:2076, and adding the r\:2076 terms makes it fall like r\:2078: the curvature terms are exactly the right ones.", "Text"],
Cell["exactMean[r0_] := NIntegrate[F0[{Sin[r0] Cos[\:03c6], Sin[r0] Sin[\:03c6], Cos[r0]}], {\:03c6, 0, 2 \:03c0}, WorkingPrecision -> 40, PrecisionGoal -> 30]/(2 \:03c0);\n{p0, d1, d2, d3} = atPole /@ {ext, L1, L2, L3};\nflat4[r_] := p0 + r^2/4 d1 + r^4 d2/64;\ncurved4[r_] := flat4[r] + r^4 d1/96;\ncurved6[r_] := curved4[r] + r^6 (d3/2304 + d2/1152 + d1/1440);\nrs = Table[2^-k, {k, 1, 6, 1/4}];\nerrs = Table[With[{ex = exactMean[r]}, {Abs[ex - flat4[r]], Abs[ex - curved4[r]], Abs[ex - curved6[r]]}], {r, rs}];\nsphereView = Show[SphericalPlot3D[1, {\:03b8, 0, \:03c0}, {\:03c6, 0, 2 \:03c0}, Mesh -> None, PlotPoints -> 50,\n    ColorFunction -> Function[{x, y, z}, ColorData[\"SunsetColors\"][Rescale[F0[{x, y, z}], {0, 12}]]], ColorFunctionScaling -> False],\n   Graphics3D[{Thick, RGBColor[0.18, 0.36, 0.78], Table[Line[Table[1.005 {Sin[r] Cos[\:03c6], Sin[r] Sin[\:03c6], Cos[r]}, {\:03c6, 0, 2 \:03c0, \:03c0/60}]], {r, 0.3, 1.5, 0.3}],\n     Black, PointSize[0.02], Point[{0, 0, 1.01}]}], Boxed -> False, Axes -> False, ViewPoint -> {2, -1.5, 1.5},\n   PlotLabel -> \"Geodesic circles about the pole\", ImageSize -> 300];\nslopes = Table[D[Fit[Log[Transpose[{rs, errs[[All, k]]}]][[-12 ;;]], {1, x}, x], x], {k, 3}];\nerrPlot = ListLogLogPlot[Table[Transpose[{rs, errs[[All, k]]}], {k, 3}], Joined -> True, PlotMarkers -> Automatic,\n   PlotStyle -> {Gray, RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17], RGBColor[0.18, 0.36, 0.78]},\n   PlotLegends -> {\"flat formula (K = 0)\", \"+ K\:0394f/96 at r^4\", \"+ r^6 curvature terms\"},\n   AxesLabel -> {\"r\", \"|error|\"}, PlotLabel -> Row[{\"error slopes \:2248 \", NumberForm[N[slopes], 3]}], ImageSize -> 440];\nRow[{sphereView, errPlot}]", "Input"],
Cell["Area of a geodesic disc divided by the flat value \:03c0r\:00b2, for K = 1 (sphere), 0 (plane) and \:22121 (hyperbolic plane). Positive curvature makes discs smaller than flat ones, negative curvature larger: the \:2212Kr\:00b2/12 term.", "Text"],
Cell["Plot[{2 (1 - Cos[r])/r^2, 1, 2 (Cosh[r] - 1)/r^2}, {r, 0.01, 2.5},\n PlotStyle -> {RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17], Gray, RGBColor[0.18, 0.36, 0.78]},\n PlotLegends -> {\"K = 1\", \"K = 0\", \"K = \:22121\"}, AxesLabel -> {\"r\", \"A(r)/\:03c0r\:00b2\"},\n PlotLabel -> \"Geodesic discs: A(r)/\:03c0r\:00b2 = 1 \:2212 Kr\:00b2/12 + \:2026\", ImageSize -> 420]", "Input"],
Cell["5. Heat kernel and spectrum: curvature is audible", "Section"],
Cell["Minakshisundaram\:2013Pleijel / McKean\:2013Singer: Tr e^{t\:0394} ~ (4plan)^{\:2212n/2} (Vol + (t/6)\:222bS + O(t\:00b2)). On the unit S\:00b2 (area 4\:03c0, S = 2) the spectrum is l(l+1) with multiplicity 2l+1, so t\:00b7Tr e^{t\:0394} \:2192 1 + t/3 + t\:00b2/15 + \:2026. For a surface the t\:2070 coefficient is (1/12\:03c0)\:222bK = \:03c7/6, so the Euler characteristic can be heard.", "Text"],
Cell["heatTrace[t_?NumericQ] := Sum[(2 j + 1) Exp[-j (j + 1) t], {j, 0, 2000}];\nTableForm[Table[{t, t heatTrace[t], 1 + t/3 + t^2/15}, {t, {0.1, 0.03, 0.01}}],\n TableHeadings -> {None, {\"t\", \"t\:00b7Tr e^{t\:0394}\", \"1 + t/3 + t\:00b2/15\"}}]", "Input"],
Cell["5.1 Two drums with the same area: the sphere and the flat torus", "Subsection"],
Cell["Normalise the heat trace by its leading Weyl term, Area/(4\:03c0t). For the unit sphere (K = 1) the ratio rises like 1 + t/3: the slope is \:222bS/(6\:00b7Area) = 1/3. For the flat square torus of the same area (K = 0) the ratio stays at 1 for small t. The first correction to Weyl\:2019s law hears the curvature.", "Text"],
Cell["L0 = 2 Sqrt[\:03c0];   (* flat torus side, so area = 4\:03c0 like the unit sphere *)\ntorusTrace[t_?NumericQ] := EllipticTheta[3, 0, Exp[-(2 \:03c0/L0)^2 t]]^2;\nratio[tr_, t_] := tr[t]/((4 \:03c0)/(4 \:03c0 t));\nPlot[{ratio[heatTrace, t], 1 + t/3 + t^2/15, ratio[torusTrace, t]}, {t, 0.005, 0.4},\n PlotStyle -> {{RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17], Thick}, {Black, Dashed}, {RGBColor[0.18, 0.36, 0.78], Thick}},\n PlotLegends -> {\"sphere S\:00b2\", \"1 + t/3 + t\:00b2/15\", \"flat torus, same area\"},\n AxesLabel -> {\"t\", \"Tr e^{t\:0394} \:00f7 (Area/4\:03c0t)\"}, PlotLabel -> \"Curvature is the first correction to Weyl\:2019s law\", ImageSize -> 460]", "Input"],
Cell["6. Bochner formula, Lichnerowicz bound and Laplacian comparison", "Section"],
Cell["Bochner: \:00bd\:0394|\:2207f|\:00b2 = |Hess f|\:00b2 + \:27e8\:2207f, \:2207\:0394f\:27e9 + Ric(\:2207f, \:2207f). This is the identity every \:201cBakry\:2013\:00c9mery\:201d curvature (Section 8) copies. Verified for an arbitrary f on S\:00b2 (Ric = g).", "Text"],
Cell["\:0394S2 = laplaceBeltrami[gS2, \|01d4cdS2];\nF = \|01d4bb[\:03b8, \:03c6];\nSimplify[1/2 \:0394S2[gradNorm2[gS2, \|01d4cdS2][F]] - (hessNorm2[gS2, \|01d4cdS2][F] +\n     Sum[Inverse[gS2][[i, j]] D[F, \|01d4cdS2[[i]]] D[\:0394S2[F], \|01d4cdS2[[j]]], {i, 2}, {j, 2}] + gradNorm2[gS2, \|01d4cdS2][F])]", "Input"],
Cell["Lichnerowicz: Ric \:2265 (n\:22121)k > 0 implies \:03bb\:2081 \:2265 nk. On the unit S\:00b2 (n = 2, k = 1) the first eigenvalue is exactly 2: equality (Obata). Laplacian comparison: Ric \:2265 0 gives \:0394r \:2264 (n\:22121)/r; on S\:00b2 the distance from the pole has \:0394r = cot r \:2264 1/r.", "Text"],
Cell["{Simplify[\:0394S2[SphericalHarmonicY[1, 0, \:03b8, \:03c6]]/SphericalHarmonicY[1, 0, \:03b8, \:03c6]],\n Simplify[\:0394S2[\:03b8]],\n And @@ Table[Cot[r] <= 1/r, {r, 0.01, 3.13, 0.01}]}", "Input"],
Cell["6.1 Worked example: Bochner on a surface whose curvature changes sign", "Subsection"],
Cell["On the torus Ric = K g with K = cos v/(2 + cos v), which is positive outside and negative inside. The Bochner identity still holds pointwise, with that variable K.", "Text"],
Cell["gTor = {{(2 + Cos[v])^2, 0}, {0, 1}}; \|01d4cdT = {u, v}; Ktor = Cos[v]/(2 + Cos[v]);\n\:0394T = laplaceBeltrami[gTor, \|01d4cdT]; FT = \|01d4bb[u, v];\nSimplify[1/2 \:0394T[gradNorm2[gTor, \|01d4cdT][FT]] - (hessNorm2[gTor, \|01d4cdT][FT] +\n    Sum[Inverse[gTor][[i, j]] D[FT, \|01d4cdT[[i]]] D[\:0394T[FT], \|01d4cdT[[j]]], {i, 2}, {j, 2}] + Ktor gradNorm2[gTor, \|01d4cdT][FT])]", "Input"],
Cell["6.2 Laplacian comparison pictured", "Subsection"],
Cell["\:0394r is the geodesic curvature of the circle of radius r: cot r for K = 1, 1/r for K = 0, coth r for K = \:22121. A lower bound on curvature caps it from above (Laplacian comparison), and integrating it gives Bishop\:2013Gromov: circumference/2\:03c0r = sin r/r, 1, sinh r/r.", "Text"],
Cell["Row[{\n  Plot[{Cot[r], 1/r, Coth[r]}, {r, 0.05, 3}, PlotRange -> {-2, 4},\n   PlotStyle -> {RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17], Gray, RGBColor[0.18, 0.36, 0.78]},\n   PlotLegends -> {\"K = 1: cot r\", \"K = 0: 1/r\", \"K = \:22121: coth r\"}, AxesLabel -> {\"r\", \"\:0394r\"}, PlotLabel -> \"\:0394r = curvature of geodesic circles\", ImageSize -> 400],\n  Plot[{Sin[r]/r, 1, Sinh[r]/r}, {r, 0.01, 3},\n   PlotStyle -> {RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17], Gray, RGBColor[0.18, 0.36, 0.78]},\n   PlotLegends -> {\"K = 1\", \"K = 0\", \"K = \:22121\"}, AxesLabel -> {\"r\", \"length/2\:03c0r\"}, PlotLabel -> \"Bishop\:2013Gromov: circle length vs flat\", ImageSize -> 320]}]", "Input"],
Cell["Lichnerowicz pictured: the first nonzero eigenvalue of S\:00b2 is 2 = nk, attained by the coordinate functions. The eigenfunction Y\:2081\:2070 = z is drawn on the sphere.", "Text"],
Cell["SphericalPlot3D[1, {\:03b8, 0, \:03c0}, {\:03c6, 0, 2 \:03c0}, Mesh -> None, PlotPoints -> 40,\n ColorFunction -> Function[{x, y, z}, \:03baColour[z, 1]], ColorFunctionScaling -> False, Boxed -> False, Axes -> False, Lighting -> \"Neutral\",\n PlotLabel -> \"\:03bb1(S\:00b2) = 2: eigenfunction z (Lichnerowicz equality)\", ImageSize -> 300]", "Input"],
Cell["7. Discrete surfaces: cotangent Laplacian, angle defect, discrete Gauss\:2013Bonnet", "Section"],
Cell["On a triangle mesh, the cotangent Laplacian (L f)_i = \:00bd \:03a3_j (cot \:03b1_ij + cot \:03b2_ij)(f_j \:2212 f_i), divided by the vertex area A_i, approximates \:0394. Applied to the vertex positions it gives the discrete mean-curvature normal (Meyer\:2013Desbrun\:2013Schr\:00f6der\:2013Barr). The angle defect 2\:03c0 \:2212 \:03a3\:03b8 at a vertex is the discrete Gauss curvature; it sums to exactly 2\:03c0\:03c7. Test: 400-point mesh of the unit sphere (H = 1, K = 1, \:03c7 = 2).", "Text"],
Cell["mesh = ConvexHullMesh[SpherePoints[400]];\nP = MeshCoordinates[mesh]; T = MeshCells[mesh, 2][[All, 1]]; nv = Length[P];\nangle[i_, j_, k_] := VectorAngle[P[[j]] - P[[i]], P[[k]] - P[[i]]];\ndefect = ConstantArray[2. \:03c0, nv]; W = SparseArray[{}, {nv, nv}, 0.]; A = ConstantArray[0., nv];\nDo[Module[{a, b, c}, {a, b, c} = tri;\n  defect[[a]] -= angle[a, b, c]; defect[[b]] -= angle[b, c, a]; defect[[c]] -= angle[c, a, b];\n  W[[b, c]] += Cot[angle[a, b, c]]/2; W[[c, b]] += Cot[angle[a, b, c]]/2;\n  W[[c, a]] += Cot[angle[b, c, a]]/2; W[[a, c]] += Cot[angle[b, c, a]]/2;\n  W[[a, b]] += Cot[angle[c, a, b]]/2; W[[b, a]] += Cot[angle[c, a, b]]/2;\n  A[[{a, b, c}]] += Norm[Cross[P[[b]] - P[[a]], P[[c]] - P[[a]]]]/6], {tri, T}];\nLcot = W - DiagonalMatrix[Total[W]];\n\:0394X = (Lcot . P)/A;\n<|\"\:03a3 angle defect / 2\:03c0 (= \:03c7)\" -> Total[defect]/(2 \:03c0),\n  \"mean K = defect/area\" -> Mean[defect/A],\n  \"mean |\:0394X|/2 (= H)\" -> Mean[Norm /@ \:0394X]/2,\n  \"mean \:0394X\:00b7n\" -> Mean[MapThread[Dot, {\:0394X, P}]]|>", "Input"],
Cell["Graphics3D[{EdgeForm[GrayLevel[.6]], MapThread[{ColorData[\"SunsetColors\"][Rescale[#2, MinMax[defect/A]]], Polygon[P[[#1]]]} &,\n   {T, Mean[(defect/A)[[#]]] & /@ T}]}, Boxed -> False, PlotLabel -> \"Angle defect / vertex area \:2248 K = 1\"]", "Input"],
Cell["7.1 Worked example: a torus mesh, where Gauss\:2013Bonnet gives zero", "Subsection"],
Cell["A 40 \:00d7 20 triangulated torus. The angle defect is positive on the outer half and negative on the inner half; its total is exactly 0 = 2\:03c0\:03c7(torus). Divided by the vertex area it approximates the smooth K = cos v/(2 + cos v), and the cotangent Laplacian of the positions approximates \:22122H\|01d427.", "Text"],
Cell["meshCurvature[P_, T_] := Module[{nv = Length[P], ang, def, Wm, Am},\n  ang[i_, j_, k_] := VectorAngle[P[[j]] - P[[i]], P[[k]] - P[[i]]];\n  def = ConstantArray[2. \:03c0, nv]; Wm = SparseArray[{}, {nv, nv}, 0.]; Am = ConstantArray[0., nv];\n  Do[Module[{a, b, c}, {a, b, c} = tri;\n    def[[a]] -= ang[a, b, c]; def[[b]] -= ang[b, c, a]; def[[c]] -= ang[c, a, b];\n    Wm[[b, c]] += Cot[ang[a, b, c]]/2; Wm[[c, b]] += Cot[ang[a, b, c]]/2;\n    Wm[[c, a]] += Cot[ang[b, c, a]]/2; Wm[[a, c]] += Cot[ang[b, c, a]]/2;\n    Wm[[a, b]] += Cot[ang[c, a, b]]/2; Wm[[b, a]] += Cot[ang[c, a, b]]/2;\n    Am[[{a, b, c}]] += Norm[Cross[P[[b]] - P[[a]], P[[c]] - P[[a]]]]/6], {tri, T}];\n  <|\"defect\" -> def, \"area\" -> Am, \"\:0394X\" -> ((Wm - DiagonalMatrix[Total[Wm]]) . P)/Am|>];\n{nu, nvv} = {40, 20}; idx[i_, j_] := Mod[i, nu] nvv + Mod[j, nvv] + 1;\nPT = Flatten[Table[N[torusPos /. {u -> 2 \:03c0 i/nu, v -> 2 \:03c0 j/nvv}], {i, 0, nu - 1}, {j, 0, nvv - 1}], 1];\nTT = Flatten[Table[{{idx[i, j], idx[i + 1, j], idx[i + 1, j + 1]}, {idx[i, j], idx[i + 1, j + 1], idx[i, j + 1]}}, {i, 0, nu - 1}, {j, 0, nvv - 1}], 2];\nmt = meshCurvature[PT, TT];\nKmesh = mt[\"defect\"]/mt[\"area\"];\nKsmooth = Flatten[Table[N[Cos[2 \:03c0 j/nvv]/(2 + Cos[2 \:03c0 j/nvv])], {i, 0, nu - 1}, {j, 0, nvv - 1}]];\n<|\"\:03a3 defect\" -> Chop[Total[mt[\"defect\"]], 10^-9], \"max |K_mesh \:2212 K|\" -> Max[Abs[Kmesh - Ksmooth]]|>", "Input"],
Cell["Legended[Row[{\n   Graphics3D[{EdgeForm[GrayLevel[0.6, 0.5]], Table[{\:03baColour[Mean[Kmesh[[t]]], 1], Polygon[PT[[t]]]}, {t, TT}]},\n    Boxed -> False, Lighting -> \"Neutral\", PlotLabel -> \"Angle defect / area \:2248 K\", ImageSize -> 340],\n   ListPlot[{Transpose[{Flatten[Table[2 \:03c0 j/nvv, {i, 0, nu - 1}, {j, 0, nvv - 1}]], Kmesh}],\n     Table[{s, Cos[s]/(2 + Cos[s])}, {s, 0, 2 \:03c0, 0.05}]}, Joined -> {False, True},\n    PlotStyle -> {RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17], Black}, AxesLabel -> {\"v\", \"K\"},\n    PlotLegends -> {\"mesh: defect/area\", \"smooth K\"}, PlotLabel -> \"Mesh vs smooth Gauss curvature\", ImageSize -> 420]}],\n \:03baLegend[1, \"K\"]]", "Input"],
Cell["8. Graph curvatures built from the graph Laplacian", "Section"],
Cell["Every notion below is defined from the graph Laplacian \:0394, from the random walk it generates, or from its pseudoinverse. Test graphs: cycle C\:2086, complete K\:2085, cube Q\:2083, Petersen graph, 4\:00d74 grid.", "Text"],
Cell["8.1 Ollivier / Lin\:2013Lu\:2013Yau curvature", "Subsection"],
Cell["Ollivier: \:03ba_\:03b1(x,y) = 1 \:2212 W\:2081(\:03bc_x^\:03b1, \:03bc_y^\:03b1)/d(x,y), with \:03bc_x^\:03b1 the lazy random walk (stay with probability \:03b1). It compares averages over two small balls \:2014 the discrete twin of Section 4. Lin\:2013Lu\:2013Yau: \:03ba = lim_{\:03b1\:21921} \:03ba_\:03b1/(1\:2212\:03b1); it equals 2\:03ba_{1/2} for every edge. M\:00fcnch\:2013Wojciechowski limit-free formula: \:03ba(x,y) = inf { \:2207_{xy}\:0394f : f 1-Lipschitz, \:2207_{yx}f = 1 }, a linear program in which the Laplacian appears explicitly. Both routes are computed and must agree.", "Text"],
Cell["nbr[G_, v_] := AdjacencyList[G, v];\n\:0394graph[G_, f_][v_] := Mean[f /@ nbr[G, v]] - f[v];\n\n\:03baLLY[G_, {x_, y_}] := Module[{S, vars, f, cons, obj},\n  S = Union[{x, y}, nbr[G, x], nbr[G, y]];\n  vars = Table[Unique[\"f\"], {Length[S]}]; f = AssociationThread[S -> vars];\n  cons = Flatten[{f[y] - f[x] == 1, f[x] == 0,\n     Table[With[{d = GraphDistance[G, S[[i]], S[[j]]]}, {f[S[[i]]] - f[S[[j]]] <= d, f[S[[j]]] - f[S[[i]]] <= d}],\n      {i, Length[S]}, {j, i + 1, Length[S]}]}];\n  obj = \:0394graph[G, f][x] - \:0394graph[G, f][y];\n  LinearOptimization[obj, cons, vars, \"PrimalMinimumValue\"]];\n\nW1[G_, \:03bc_Association, \:03bd_Association] := Module[{A = Keys[\:03bc], B = Keys[\:03bd], plan, vars},\n  vars = Flatten@Table[plan[a, b], {a, A}, {b, B}];\n  LinearOptimization[Sum[GraphDistance[G, a, b] plan[a, b], {a, A}, {b, B}],\n   Join[Table[Sum[plan[a, b], {b, B}] == \:03bc[a], {a, A}], Table[Sum[plan[a, b], {a, A}] == \:03bd[b], {b, B}], Thread[vars >= 0]],\n   vars, \"PrimalMinimumValue\"]];\n\:03bcLazy[G_, x_, \:03b1_] := Merge[{<|x -> \:03b1|>, AssociationThread[nbr[G, x] -> (1 - \:03b1)/Length[nbr[G, x]]]}, Total];\n\:03baOllivier[G_, {x_, y_}, \:03b1_] := 1 - W1[G, \:03bcLazy[G, x, \:03b1], \:03bcLazy[G, y, \:03b1]];\n\ngraphs = <|\"C6\" -> CycleGraph[6], \"K5\" -> CompleteGraph[5], \"Q3\" -> HypercubeGraph[3],\n   \"Petersen\" -> PetersenGraph[], \"Grid4x4\" -> GridGraph[{4, 4}]|>;\nedges[G_] := List @@@ EdgeList[G];\nDataset@Map[Function[G, <|\"min \:03ba_LLY (LP)\" -> Min[\:03baLLY[G, #] & /@ edges[G]],\n     \"min 2\:03ba_1/2 (transport)\" -> Min[2 \:03baOllivier[G, #, 1/2] & /@ edges[G]],\n     \"min \:03ba_0\" -> Min[\:03baOllivier[G, #, 0] & /@ edges[G]]|>], graphs]", "Input"],
Cell["8.2 Bakry\:2013\:00c9mery curvature (the discrete Bochner formula)", "Subsection"],
Cell["\:0393(f) = \:00bd(\:0394f\:00b2 \:2212 2f\:0394f), \:0393\:2082(f) = \:00bd(\:0394\:0393(f) \:2212 2\:0393(f, \:0394f)). The curvature-dimension condition CD(K, \:221e) at x is \:0393\:2082(f)(x) \:2265 K \:0393(f)(x) for all f. Both sides are quadratic forms in the values of f on the 2-ball of x, so the best K is the largest K with \:0393\:2082 \:2212 K\:0393 positive semidefinite (found by bisection).", "Text"],
Cell["bakryEmery[G_, x_] := Module[{B2, F, ff, \:0393v, q1, q2, M1, M2, lo = -10., hi = 10., mid},\n  B2 = VertexList[NeighborhoodGraph[G, x, 2]];\n  F = Array[\|01d4bb, Length[B2]]; ff = AssociationThread[B2 -> F];\n  \:0393v[v_] := 1/2 Mean[(ff[#] - ff[v])^2 & /@ nbr[G, v]];\n  q1 = \:0393v[x];\n  q2 = 1/2 (Mean[\:0393v /@ nbr[G, x]] - \:0393v[x]) -\n    1/2 Mean[(ff[#] - ff[x]) (\:0394graph[G, ff][#] - \:0394graph[G, ff][x]) & /@ nbr[G, x]];\n  M1 = D[q1, {F, 2}]/2; M2 = D[q2, {F, 2}]/2;\n  Do[mid = (lo + hi)/2; If[Min[Eigenvalues[N[M2 - mid M1]]] >= -10^-9, lo = mid, hi = mid], {60}];\n  N[Floor[lo 10^6]/10^6]];   (* rounded down, so theorem checks stay safe *)\nDataset@Map[Function[G, Min[bakryEmery[G, #] & /@ VertexList[G]]], graphs]", "Input"],
Cell["8.3 Forman curvature (the discrete Bochner\:2013Weitzenb\:00f6ck splitting)", "Subsection"],
Cell["Forman splits the combinatorial Hodge Laplacian on 1-cells as \:25a1 = B + F; the diagonal part F is the curvature. For an unweighted graph, F(e) = 4 \:2212 d_x \:2212 d_y; counting triangles as 2-cells gives the augmented version 4 \:2212 d_x \:2212 d_y + 3\:00b7#triangles(e).", "Text"],
Cell["forman[G_, {x_, y_}] := 4 - VertexDegree[G, x] - VertexDegree[G, y];\nformanAug[G_, {x_, y_}] := forman[G, {x, y}] + 3 Length[Intersection[nbr[G, x], nbr[G, y]]];\nDataset@Map[Function[G, <|\"min Forman\" -> Min[forman[G, #] & /@ edges[G]],\n     \"min augmented\" -> Min[formanAug[G, #] & /@ edges[G]]|>], graphs]", "Input"],
Cell["8.4 Resistance curvature (from the pseudoinverse of the Laplacian)", "Subsection"],
Cell["Effective resistance \:03c9_ij = (L\:207a)_ii + (L\:207a)_jj \:2212 2(L\:207a)_ij with L the Kirchhoff matrix. Node curvature p_i = 1 \:2212 \:00bd \:03a3_{j~i} \:03c9_ij (Devriendt\:2013Lambiotte). Foster\:2019s theorem \:03a3_edges \:03c9 = n \:2212 1 makes \:03a3 p_i = 1 for every connected graph: a discrete Gauss\:2013Bonnet theorem. Edge curvature \:03ba_ij = 2(p_i + p_j)/\:03c9_ij.", "Text"],
Cell["resistance[G_] := Module[{Lp = PseudoInverse[N@KirchhoffMatrix[G]], n = VertexCount[G]},\n  Table[Lp[[i, i]] + Lp[[j, j]] - 2 Lp[[i, j]], {i, n}, {j, n}]];\npNode[G_] := With[{\:03a9 = resistance[G], V = VertexList[G]},\n  Table[1 - 1/2 Sum[\:03a9[[i, VertexIndex[G, j]]], {j, nbr[G, V[[i]]]}], {i, Length[V]}]];\nDataset@Map[<|\"\:03a3 p (=1)\" -> Chop@Total[pNode[#]], \"min p\" -> Min[pNode[#]], \"max p\" -> Max[pNode[#]]|> &, graphs]", "Input"],
Cell["8.5 Steinerberger curvature and combinatorial (Higuchi) curvature", "Subsection"],
Cell["Steinerberger: solve D\:00b7K = n\:00b71 with D the distance matrix (least squares if singular). Higuchi\:2019s combinatorial curvature for a polyhedral surface: \:03ba(v) = 1 \:2212 d_v/2 + \:03a3_faces\:220bv 1/|face|; by Euler\:2019s formula it sums to \:03c7 = 2 on every convex polyhedron.", "Text"],
Cell["steinerberger[G_] := LeastSquares[N@GraphDistanceMatrix[G], ConstantArray[N@VertexCount[G], VertexCount[G]]];\nhiguchiTotal[name_] := Module[{F = PolyhedronData[name, \"FaceIndices\"], nv = Length[PolyhedronData[name, \"VertexCoordinates\"]]},\n  Total@Table[1 - Count[F, _?(MemberQ[#, v] &)]/2 + Total[1/Length[#] & /@ Select[F, MemberQ[#, v] &]], {v, nv}]];\n{Dataset@Map[<|\"min K\" -> Min[steinerberger[#]], \"max K\" -> Max[steinerberger[#]]|> &, graphs],\n AssociationMap[higuchiTotal, {\"Tetrahedron\", \"Cube\", \"Dodecahedron\", \"Icosahedron\", \"TruncatedIcosahedron\"}]}", "Input"],
Cell["8.6 Pictures: graphs coloured by their curvature", "Subsection"],
Cell["One drawing helper, then each curvature on a family of examples. The family: a binary tree (very negative), the 6-cycle (flat), the 3-cube, the complete graph K\:2085 (positive), the Petersen graph, the 5\:00d75 grid with boundary, and a dumbbell (two K\:2085 joined by a path), whose bridge is a bottleneck. Edge curvatures colour the edges; vertex curvatures colour the vertices. Blue = negative, orange = positive.", "Text"],
Cell["dumbbell = Graph[UndirectedEdge @@@ Join[List @@@ EdgeList[CompleteGraph[5]], (List @@@ EdgeList[CompleteGraph[5]]) + 7, {{5, 6}, {6, 7}, {7, 8}}]];\nfamily = <|\"Binary tree\" -> KaryTree[15, 2], \"Cycle C6\" -> CycleGraph[6], \"Cube Q3\" -> HypercubeGraph[3],\n   \"Complete K5\" -> CompleteGraph[5], \"Petersen\" -> PetersenGraph[], \"Grid 5\:00d75\" -> GridGraph[{5, 5}], \"Dumbbell\" -> dumbbell|>;\nedgeCurvaturePlot[G_, \:03baf_, s_, label_] := Module[{e = edges[G], k},\n  k = AssociationThread[e -> (\:03baf[G, #] & /@ e)];\n  Graph[G, EdgeStyle -> Table[UndirectedEdge @@ ed -> Directive[AbsoluteThickness[4], \:03baColour[k[ed], s]], {ed, e}],\n   VertexStyle -> GrayLevel[0.35], VertexSize -> {\"Scaled\", 0.025}, PlotLabel -> Style[label, 10], ImageSize -> 180]];\nvertexCurvaturePlot[G_, kv_List, s_, label_] := Graph[G,\n  VertexStyle -> Thread[VertexList[G] -> (\:03baColour[#, s] & /@ kv)], VertexSize -> {\"Scaled\", 0.05},\n  EdgeStyle -> GrayLevel[0.6], PlotLabel -> Style[label, 10], ImageSize -> 180];\nrow[plots_] := GraphicsRow[plots, ImageSize -> 1100, Spacings -> 0];\nKeys[family]", "Input"],
Cell["Lin\:2013Lu\:2013Yau curvature on every edge. In the binary tree, interior edges are \:22122/3 (the two neighbourhoods do not overlap) while leaf edges are +2/3 (a leaf sees only its parent). The cycle and the Petersen graph are 0, the cube is 2/3 everywhere and K\:2085 is 5/4. The grid is 0 inside and positive along the boundary. The dumbbell\:2019s bridge is negative (\:22123/5) while its cliques are positive: the bottleneck shows up as negative curvature.", "Text"],
Cell["Legended[row[KeyValueMap[Function[{nm, G}, edgeCurvaturePlot[G, \:03baLLY, 1.25, nm]], family]], \:03baLegend[1.25, \"\:03ba_LLY\"]]", "Input"],
Cell["Dataset[Map[Function[G, With[{k = \:03baLLY[G, #] & /@ edges[G]}, <|\"min\" -> Min[k], \"max\" -> Max[k], \"mean\" -> N[Mean[k]]|>]], family]]", "Input"],
Cell["What Ollivier\:2019s curvature measures, on one edge: the walk measures \:03bc_x and \:03bc_y spread mass over the two neighbourhoods. Shared neighbours (purple, the triangles) let mass stay put, which is why K\:2085 is positive. Across an interior tree edge the neighbourhoods do not overlap, mass must travel up to 3 steps, and \:03ba is negative.", "Text"],
Cell["edgePicture[G_, {x_, y_}, label_] := Graph[G,\n  VertexStyle -> Join[Thread[Union[nbr[G, x], nbr[G, y]] -> RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17]],\n    Thread[Intersection[nbr[G, x], nbr[G, y]] -> RGBColor[0.55, 0.3, 0.75]], {x -> Black, y -> Black}],\n  EdgeStyle -> {UndirectedEdge[x, y] -> Directive[Black, AbsoluteThickness[4]]}, VertexSize -> {\"Scaled\", 0.05},\n  PlotLabel -> Style[Row[{label, \":  \:03ba_LLY(x,y) = \", \:03baLLY[G, {x, y}], \",  \:03ba\:2080 = \", \:03baOllivier[G, {x, y}, 0]}], 10], ImageSize -> 260];\nGraphicsRow[{edgePicture[KaryTree[15, 2], {2, 4}, \"tree, interior edge\"], edgePicture[HypercubeGraph[3], {1, 2}, \"cube\"],\n  edgePicture[CompleteGraph[5], {1, 2}, \"K5 (purple = shared)\"]}, ImageSize -> 780]", "Input"],
Cell["Bakry\:2013\:00c9mery curvature K\:221e at every vertex (the discrete Bochner inequality). Tree vertices with children are negative and leaves positive; the cycle is exactly 0; the cube is 2/3, K\:2085 is 7/8 and Petersen is \:22121/3 at every vertex; on the dumbbell the bridge vertices are negative.", "Text"],
Cell["Legended[row[KeyValueMap[Function[{nm, G}, vertexCurvaturePlot[G, bakryEmery[G, #] & /@ VertexList[G], 1, nm]], family]], \:03baLegend[1, \"K_BE\"]]", "Input"],
Cell["Forman curvature (augmented with triangles) on every edge. It is purely combinatorial, so it is very negative wherever degrees are high and triangles are few.", "Text"],
Cell["Legended[row[KeyValueMap[Function[{nm, G}, edgeCurvaturePlot[G, formanAug, 6, nm]], family]], \:03baLegend[6, \"Forman\"]]", "Input"],
Cell["Resistance curvature p at every vertex. The totals are always 1, a discrete Gauss\:2013Bonnet theorem; the dumbbell\:2019s bridge vertices carry the negative curvature.", "Text"],
Cell["{Legended[row[KeyValueMap[Function[{nm, G}, vertexCurvaturePlot[G, pNode[G], 0.3, nm]], family]], \:03baLegend[0.3, \"p\"]],\n Map[Chop@Total[pNode[#]] &, family]}", "Input"],
Cell["Steinerberger curvature at every vertex. It is built from graph distance where resistance curvature uses effective resistance. On a tree the two distances coincide, so the two curvatures are exactly proportional; on the grid they are strongly correlated; on vertex-transitive graphs (cycle, cube, K\:2085, Petersen) both are constant.", "Text"],
Cell["{Legended[row[KeyValueMap[Function[{nm, G}, vertexCurvaturePlot[G, steinerberger[G], 1.5, nm]], family]], \:03baLegend[1.5, \"K_S\"]],\n \"correlation with resistance curvature\" -> Map[Quiet@Correlation[steinerberger[#], pNode[#]] &, KeyTake[family, {\"Binary tree\", \"Grid 5\:00d75\", \"Dumbbell\"}]]}", "Input"],
Cell["Combinatorial (Higuchi) curvature drawn on polyhedra: every vertex of a Platonic solid carries the same positive share, and the shares add to \:03c7 = 2.", "Text"],
Cell["higuchiVertex[name_] := Module[{F = PolyhedronData[name, \"FaceIndices\"], nv = Length[PolyhedronData[name, \"VertexCoordinates\"]]},\n   Table[1 - Count[F, _?(MemberQ[#, v] &)]/2 + Total[1/Length[#] & /@ Select[F, MemberQ[#, v] &]], {v, nv}]];\nGraphicsRow[Table[Show[PolyhedronData[p], PlotLabel -> Style[Row[{p, \": \", First[higuchiVertex[p]], \" \:00d7 \", Length[higuchiVertex[p]], \" = 2\"}], 9]],\n   {p, {\"Tetrahedron\", \"Cube\", \"Octahedron\", \"Dodecahedron\", \"Icosahedron\"}}], ImageSize -> 900]", "Input"],
Cell["9. Spectral consequences on graphs, and the graph-to-manifold limit", "Section"],
Cell["Discrete Lichnerowicz theorems for the normalised Laplacian I \:2212 D^{\:22121/2}AD^{\:22121/2}: Ollivier curvature \:2265 \:03ba > 0 gives \:03bb\:2081 \:2265 \:03ba (Ollivier; Lin\:2013Lu\:2013Yau); CD(K,\:221e) gives \:03bb\:2081 \:2265 K (Bakry\:2013\:00c9mery; Lin\:2013Yau); and \:03bb_max \:2264 2 \:2212 \:03ba\:2080 (Ollivier, Bauer\:2013Jost\:2013Liu).", "Text"],
Cell["normLap[G_] := Module[{A = N@AdjacencyMatrix[G], d}, d = Total[A];\n   IdentityMatrix[Length[d]] - DiagonalMatrix[d^(-1/2)] . A . DiagonalMatrix[d^(-1/2)]];\nspec[G_] := Sort[Eigenvalues[normLap[G]]];\nDataset@Map[Function[G, With[{s = spec[G], kL = Min[\:03baLLY[G, #] & /@ edges[G]], kB = Min[bakryEmery[G, #] & /@ VertexList[G]],\n      k0 = Min[\:03baOllivier[G, #, 0] & /@ edges[G]]},\n     <|\"\:03bb1\" -> s[[2]], \"\:03bbmax\" -> Last[s], \"\:03bb1 \:2265 \:03ba_LLY\" -> s[[2]] >= kL - 10^-9,\n       \"\:03bb1 \:2265 K_BE\" -> s[[2]] >= kB - 10^-6, \"\:03bbmax \:2264 2 \:2212 \:03ba0\" -> Last[s] <= 2 - k0 + 10^-9|>]], graphs]", "Input"],
Cell["Belkin\:2013Niyogi: on points sampled from a manifold, the heat-kernel graph Laplacian (weighted mean \:2212 value)/t converges to \:0394. Check on 20 000 points of S\:00b2 with f = z, for which \:0394f = \:22122f: the fitted ratio should be close to 1.", "Text"],
Cell["pts = SpherePoints[20000]; t = 0.002; nf = Nearest[pts];\nLpc[p_] := Module[{q = nf[p, {All, 6 Sqrt[t]}], w}, w = Exp[-SquaredEuclideanDistance[p, #]/(4 t)] & /@ q;\n   (w . q[[All, 3]]/Total[w] - p[[3]])/t];\ns = pts[[;; ;; 200]]; lhs = Lpc /@ s; rhs = -2 s[[All, 3]];\n<|\"fitted ratio\" -> (lhs . rhs)/(rhs . rhs), \"correlation\" -> Correlation[lhs, rhs]|>", "Input"],
Cell["9.1 Pictures: curvature lower bounds under the spectral gap", "Subsection"],
Cell["For each graph, \:03bb\:2081 of the normalised Laplacian against the two curvature lower bounds. Every bar of \:03ba_LLY and K_BE sits at or below \:03bb\:2081; K\:2085 and Q\:2083 touch it, the discrete equality cases.", "Text"],
Cell["bounds = Map[Function[G, {spec[G][[2]], Max[0, Min[\:03baLLY[G, #] & /@ edges[G]]], Max[0, Min[bakryEmery[G, #] & /@ VertexList[G]]]}], graphs];\nBarChart[Values[bounds], ChartLabels -> {Keys[bounds], None}, ChartStyle -> {Black, RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17], RGBColor[0.18, 0.36, 0.78]},\n ChartLegends -> {\"\:03bb\:2081\", \"max(0, \:03ba_LLY)\", \"max(0, K_BE)\"}, PlotLabel -> \"\:03bb\:2081 \:2265 \:03ba and \:03bb\:2081 \:2265 K\", ImageSize -> 520]", "Input"],
Cell["The point-cloud Laplacian of f = z against the exact \:0394z = \:22122z at the sampled points: they lie on the diagonal.", "Text"],
Cell["ListPlot[Transpose[{rhs, lhs}], AspectRatio -> 1, PlotStyle -> RGBColor[0.98, 0.56, 0.17],\n Epilog -> {Gray, Dashed, Line[{{-2, -2}, {2, 2}}]}, AxesLabel -> {\"\:0394z = \:22122z\", \"point-cloud L z\"},\n PlotLabel -> \"Belkin\:2013Niyogi on 20 000 points of S\:00b2\", ImageSize -> 320]", "Input"],
Cell["10. Further instances (statements only)", "Section"],
Cell["\:2022 Weitzenb\:00f6ck on 1-forms: \:0394_Hodge \:03c9 = \:2207*\:2207\:03c9 + Ric(\:03c9); Bochner\:2019s theorem (Ric > 0 \:21d2 b\:2081 = 0) follows. \:2022 Ricci flow \:2202g/\:2202t = \:22122Ric: in dimension 2 with g = e^{2u}g\:2080 it reads \:2202u/\:2202t = e^{\:22122u}\:0394\:2080u = \:2212K, a nonlinear heat equation. \:2022 Mean curvature flow \:2202X/\:2202t = \:0394X = \:22122H\|01d427: the heat equation for the embedding. \:2022 Ollivier curvature on a manifold: for nearby x, y along v, \:03ba \:2248 \:03b5\:00b2 Ric(v,v)/(2(n+2)) for balls of radius \:03b5 \:2014 Ricci curvature measured by comparing ball means. \:2022 Yamabe / prescribed scalar curvature: for g\:0303 = u^{4/(n\:22122)}g, \:2212(4(n\:22121)/(n\:22122))\:0394u + Su = S\:0303u^{(n+2)/(n\:22122)}.", "Text"],
Cell["\:00a9 2026 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"]
},
WindowTitle -> "The Laplacian and Curvature",
StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"
]
